{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792773062,"nonce":"Gwjhd6nnu+l2fxKczH+kII6qyOc=","title":"Compiler: module globals of dynamic array type hold the list header by value","message":"A typed-AST `TypedDeclaration::Variable` whose type is `Type::Array { size: None }` is declared by `lower_global_variable` with `convert_type`, so the HIR global is `%List{ i64, i64, i64 }` by value. Reads through `global_slot` then `load` that struct and use the loaded value as the header address (`gep %v13, [8]`, `store %v27, %v13`). The array assigned to the global is lost: pushes and length reads see an empty list. List elements already avoid this with `element_storage_type`, which stores a dynamic array as `Ptr(List header)`; a global of array type needs the same storage so that every reader shares the header's identity and growth.\n\nBlocks TypeScript module bindings of array type used in functions (git-bug 751af73). The TypeScript frontend reports those as Unsupported until this lands; scalars, static strings and class instances already work as globals.\n\nReproducer (frozen frontend build with module globals, before the Unsupported gate was added):\n\n    /private/tmp/ts-global-repro/zynode-globals-wip run /private/tmp/ts-global-repro/array_global.ts\n\nSource:\n\n    const data: number[] = [];\n    function add(x: number): void { data.push(x); }\n    data.push(1);\n    add(2);\n    console.log(data.length, data[0], data[1]);\n\nExpected `2 1 2`. Actual: `RangeError: array index 0 is outside 0..0`, exit 1, in the interpreter and with `--llvm` (the failure happens in the first tier). HIR dumps: /private/tmp/ts-global-repro/hir (`global @...global$data: %List{ i64, i64, i64 }`; see `fn$add`, block1).","files":null}]}